---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import TermCapture from '../../components/TermCapture.astro';
import OpsArchitecture from '../../components/OpsArchitecture.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith('/') ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const repo = 'https://github.com/BusySloths/mlox';

const chapters = [
  ['setup', 'The setup'],
  ['services', 'Five services, wired together'],
  ['operations', 'The Operations view'],
  ['start', 'Start watching, start traffic'],
  ['pipelines', 'How MLOX sees a pipeline'],
  ['calibrate', 'Calibrate'],
  ['break', 'Break it'],
  ['detect', 'Detect'],
  ['investigate', 'Investigate'],
  ['recover', 'Recover'],
  ['try', 'Try it yourself'],
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Catching a silent model failure, live · MLOX demo"
  description="A model on Kubernetes starts giving wrong answers without throwing an error. MLOX discovers the pipeline from telemetry, flags the broken step and lets a reasoning model explain it. An experimental feature, shown on real infrastructure."
>
  <Header />

  <main id="main">
    <!-- ============ INTRO ============ -->
    <header class="intro">
      <div class="wrap intro__grid">
        <div class="intro__copy">
          <a class="back mono rise rise-1" href={`${base}learn/`}><span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Tutorials &amp; demos</a>
          <p class="eyebrow rise rise-1">Demo · Operations · 8 min read</p>
          <h1 class="h-display intro__title rise rise-2">Catching a <span class="serif accent">silent</span> model failure, live.</h1>
          <p class="lede rise rise-3">
            A model served from Kubernetes starts giving wrong answers without throwing a single error. MLOX notices within a
            few requests, points at the step inside the model that broke, and a reasoning model explains what happened and
            proposes a fix.
          </p>
          <ul class="chips mono rise rise-4">
            <li>live infrastructure</li>
            <li>no hand-written rules</li>
            <li>two-step Reasonable AI</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <aside class="exp rise rise-4" aria-label="Status of this feature">
          <p class="exp__k mono">Experimental feature</p>
          <p>
            Everything shown here is real code in MLOX. It ships since <strong>v0.5.0</strong> as the
            <strong>Operations (experimental)</strong> tab of the terminal UI, together with the demo in
            <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples/operations_demo`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><code>examples/operations_demo</code></a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is a technology preview. Interfaces, defaults and behaviour will change, and it is
            <strong>not ready for production environments yet</strong>. Try it on a test project, not on systems you depend on.
          </p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- ============ WHY ============ -->
    <section class="prose-section">
      <div class="wrap prose-grid">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2 class="h-sub">The failures that don't crash</h2>
          <p>
            The hardest problems in machine learning rarely announce themselves. A model behind an API keeps answering
            <code>200 OK</code> while its predictions drift: a preprocessing step changed, a dependency was upgraded, a feature
            now arrives on a different scale. Health checks stay green, dashboards show the usual traffic and latency, and the
            first one to notice is often a customer.
          </p>
          <p>
            This demo shows how MLOX approaches that problem on infrastructure you run yourself. We serve a small model from a
            real Kubernetes cluster, send it realistic traffic, and then break one step inside it on purpose. Nobody tells MLOX
            about the fault. It has to find it from the telemetry the model already emits.
          </p>
          <p>
            The approach has two steps, which we call <em>Reasonable AI</em>. Fast early-detection models watch every step of
            every request and raise an incident within seconds. A reasoning model then investigates the incident, explains it in
            plain language and proposes concrete actions inside MLOX. Neither step needs hand-written rules or a hand-drawn
            pipeline.
          </p>
        </div>
        <nav class="toc" aria-label="Chapters">
          <p class="toc__k mono">In this demo</p>
          <ol>
            {chapters.map(([id, title], i) => (
              <li><a href={`#${id}`}><span class="mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>{title}</a></li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 01 SETUP ============ -->
    <section id="setup" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">01</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">One window for the <span class="serif accent">users</span>, one for the <span class="serif accent">operator</span>.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter__text">
          <p>
            The demo runs in two terminal tabs. On the left, a small traffic simulator plays the users: every two seconds it
            sends a batch of prediction requests with realistic noise to the model, and labels so the prediction error can be
            measured. It also has a corruption switch. Typing <code>on</code> breaks one step inside the model, <code>off</code>
            repairs it. The fault itself is never reported anywhere, so MLOX has no shortcut.
          </p>
          <p>
            On the right is the MLOX terminal UI, the operator's control room. Its tree shows the whole project: the servers,
            the services running on each of them, and the Operations tab we will use for the rest of the demo.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="duo">
          <TermCapture
            name="setup_traffic"
            cols={84}
            max={13}
            title="tab · demo-traffic"
            label="The traffic simulator after start: a help panel with the on/off commands, a status panel showing the gateway, registry, telemetry and secret manager, and the first successful requests."
            callouts={[{ kind: 'mark', r: 17, c: 50, label: '1' }, { kind: 'mark', r: 3, c: 62, label: '2', tone: 'red' }]}
          />
          <TermCapture
            name="setup_tui"
            cols={219}
            max={8}
            title="tab · demo-tui"
            label="The MLOX terminal UI showing the project tree with servers and services."
            callouts={[{ kind: 'mark', r: 8, c: 44, label: '3' }]}
          />
        </div>
        <ol class="legend-list">
          <li><span class="n">1</span><b>User simulation.</b> Batches of prediction requests, every two seconds.</li>
          <li><span class="n red">2</span><b>Corruption switch.</b> <code>on</code> breaks a step, <code>off</code> repairs it.</li>
          <li><span class="n">3</span><b>The control room.</b> Servers, services and the Operations tab in one tree.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 02 SERVICES ============ -->
    <section id="services" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">02</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Five services, wired together <span class="serif accent">by MLOX</span>.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter__text">
          <p>
            Everything in this demo was deployed by MLOX from its service catalog. The model is served by an
            <strong>MLflow Gateway</strong> on a k3s cluster. It loads whichever model version carries the <code>champion</code>
            alias in the <strong>MLflow</strong> registry, gets its credentials from <strong>OpenBao</strong>, and exports a trace
            for every request to an <strong>OpenTelemetry Collector</strong>, which stores it in <strong>InfluxDB</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The important part is what nobody had to do. When a service is added to an MLOX project it is bound to the services
            it depends on, so the gateway already knows its registry, its collector and its secret store. No URLs or passwords
            were copied between configuration files to set this up.
          </p>
        </div>
        <OpsArchitecture />
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 03 OPERATIONS ============ -->
    <section id="operations" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">03</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">One tab for <span class="serif accent">watching</span>, planning and recovering.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              The Operations tab is where the observer lives. It has five focused views: <em>Team</em> (the AI assistant),
              <em>Pipelines</em> (live health), <em>Plan</em>, <em>Activity</em> log and <em>Settings</em>. Setting up a watch
              takes three choices.
            </p>
            <ol class="legend-list legend-list--stacked">
              <li><span class="n">1</span><b>Settings.</b> The view where a watch is configured.</li>
              <li><span class="n">2</span><b>Telemetry source.</b> The database MLOX reads the gateway's traces from. Any monitor in the project can be chosen.</li>
              <li><span class="n">3</span><b>Start watching.</b> From now on, every completed request becomes evidence.</li>
              <li><span class="n amber">4</span><b>The reasoning model.</b> Step two of Reasonable AI: a pluggable slot for any model behind a standard API, self-hosted or hosted, built for our own efficient reasoning models. Its key stays in the project's secret store.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <TermCapture
            name="ops_settings"
            cols={187}
            max={11}
            title="mlox tui · Operations › Settings"
            label="Operations settings: the telemetry monitor selection, Start Watching, Stop Watching and Reset buttons, and the reasoning assistant configuration."
            callouts={[
              { kind: 'mark', r: 16, c: 3, label: '1' },
              { kind: 'ring', r: 15, c: 27, w: 157, h: 3 },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 16, c: 25, label: '2' },
              { kind: 'ring', r: 22, c: 28, w: 50, h: 3 },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 23, c: 25, label: '3' },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 26, c: 25, label: '4', tone: 'amber' },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 04 START ============ -->
    <section id="start" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">04</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Press <span class="serif accent">Start Watching</span>, then let the users in.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter__text">
          <p>
            A watch always starts from a clean slate. Telemetry that arrived before the button was pressed is ignored, so the
            baseline only ever reflects what happens from this moment on. Then the traffic simulator starts sending requests,
            and a few seconds later the first complete trace arrives: the pipeline appears in the <em>Pipelines</em> view and
            starts calibrating.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="duo duo--wide-right">
          <TermCapture
            name="start_traffic"
            cols={84}
            max={13}
            title="tab · demo-traffic"
            label="The traffic simulator sending its first requests with fault off and labels on."
          />
          <TermCapture
            name="start_pipelines"
            cols={149}
            max={11}
            title="mlox tui · Operations › Pipelines"
            label="The Pipelines view with the newly discovered operations-demonstrator workload in the calibrating state."
            callouts={[{ kind: 'ring', r: 7, c: 89, w: 14, h: 1 }]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 05 PIPELINES ============ -->
    <section id="pipelines" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">05</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Pipelines are <span class="serif accent">discovered</span>, not drawn by hand.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="split split--code">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              The served model reports each step it runs as a span, and all steps of one request share a trace. MLOX assembles
              those traces into a pipeline and keeps, for every step, how long it took and what its output looked like. Nobody
              configures the structure: add a step to the model and it appears in the pipeline with the next request.
            </p>
            <p>
              Instrumenting a model takes a few lines in its <code>live_predict</code> method. Each step is a context manager,
              and <code>observe_array</code> records a compact summary of the step's output, not the data itself.
            </p>
          </div>
          <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="k">def</span> live_predict(self, model_input, params=None, artifacts=None):
    <span class="c"># … unpack features</span>
    <span class="k">with</span> self.model_step(<span class="s">"pipeline"</span>, kind=<span class="s">"pipeline"</span>):
        <span class="k">with</span> self.model_step(<span class="s">"input.normalize"</span>) <span class="k">as</span> step:
            normalized = (features - self.mean) / self.scale
            step.observe_array(<span class="s">"output"</span>, normalized)

        <span class="k">with</span> self.model_step(<span class="s">"pca.transform"</span>) <span class="k">as</span> step:
            transformed = normalized @ self.components
            step.observe_array(<span class="s">"output"</span>, transformed)

        <span class="c"># regression.predict, quality.evaluate …</span></code></pre>
        </div>
        <div class="pipe" role="list" aria-label="The demo pipeline">
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">input.normalize<small>scales the raw features</small></div>
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">pca.transform<small>compresses them to principal components</small></div>
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">regression.predict<small>turns components into a prediction</small></div>
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">quality.evaluate<small>compares predictions with labels (RMSE)</small></div>
        </div>
        <div class="tiers">
          <article class="tier">
            <p class="tier__k mono">Step 1 · seconds</p>
            <h3>Early-detection anomaly models</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Fast statistical and ML detectors watch every step and signal.</li>
              <li>One-class models learn normal from historical runs, no labels needed.</li>
              <li>Short and long windows catch both sudden and creeping change.</li>
              <li>Deviations are traced through the pipeline graph to their origin.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="tier tier--deep">
            <p class="tier__k mono">Step 2 · automatic or on demand</p>
            <h3>Reasoning: investigation and mitigation</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Starts as soon as step 1 raises an incident, or when an operator asks.</li>
              <li>Reasons over the incident subgraph: baselines, deviations, likely origin.</li>
              <li>Returns a diagnosis, a recovery check and real MLOX actions.</li>
              <li>Routine actions run on their own; critical ones wait for approval.</li>
            </ul>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 06 CALIBRATE ============ -->
    <section id="calibrate" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">06</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Ten normal runs <span class="serif accent">teach</span> MLOX what healthy looks like.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              The first ten complete runs of a pipeline become its baseline. That is deliberately small: the demo only needs
              about twenty seconds of normal traffic before it can tell normal from abnormal. Once the baseline is full, the
              state switches from calibrating to watching.
            </p>
            <ol class="legend-list legend-list--stacked">
              <li><span class="n">1</span><b>Baseline 10 / 10.</b> The first ten runs define normal.</li>
              <li><span class="n">2</span><b>Watching.</b> Every new request is compared with the baseline, step by step.</li>
              <li><span class="n">3</span><b>All steps normal.</b> Each step is checked over a 1-minute and a 5-minute window. The prediction error (RMSE) sits at about 0.02.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <TermCapture
            name="calibrated"
            cols={149}
            max={11}
            title="mlox tui · Operations › Pipelines"
            label="The pipeline in the watching state with a full baseline of ten runs; all four steps are normal."
            callouts={[
              { kind: 'mark', r: 7, c: 67, label: '1' },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 7, c: 102, label: '2' },
              { kind: 'ring', r: 16, c: 2, w: 91, h: 6 },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 21, c: 97, label: '3' },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 07 BREAK ============ -->
    <section id="break" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">07</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">One command, one <span class="serif accent">hidden</span> fault.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="split split--small">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              Typing <code>on</code> shifts the first principal component inside <code>pca.transform</code>, the kind of
              mistake a bad refit or a changed preprocessing library could cause. Every request still returns
              <code>200 OK</code>. Latency is unchanged. Only the answers are wrong.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
              <b>What MLOX is not told:</b> the fault flag is never part of the telemetry. MLOX has to work out where the problem
              is from the step observations alone.
            </p>
          </div>
          <TermCapture
            name="corrupt_on"
            cols={54}
            max={17}
            title="tab · demo-traffic"
            label="The traffic simulator after typing on: fault injection is now on and requests continue to succeed."
          />
        </div>
        <div class="pipe" role="list" aria-label="Where the fault sits">
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">input.normalize<small>unchanged</small></div>
          <div class="stage bad" role="listitem">pca.transform<small>first component shifted</small></div>
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">regression.predict<small>receives bad inputs</small></div>
          <div class="stage" role="listitem">quality.evaluate<small>error climbs</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 08 DETECT ============ -->
    <section id="detect" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">08</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">MLOX points at <span class="serif accent">pca.transform</span>, not just “error went up”.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="chapter__text">
          <p>
            Two requests after the fault, the pipeline switches from watching to incident. The output of
            <code>pca.transform</code> jumped from a baseline of 2.79 to 14.55, while <code>input.normalize</code>, the step
            before it, stayed normal. That contrast is what makes the origin credible: the problem starts where healthy inputs
            produce unhealthy outputs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Seconds later the damage spreads downstream. <code>regression.predict</code> receives bad components and its
            predictions drift, and <code>quality.evaluate</code> sees the error rise from 0.02 to 2.4, a hundredfold. An alert
            on the error alone would have told you <em>that</em> something broke. The pipeline view tells you <em>where</em>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <TermCapture
          name="incident"
          cols={149}
          max={13}
          title="mlox tui · Operations › Pipelines"
          label="The pipeline in incident: pca.transform, regression.predict and quality.evaluate are anomalous; the likely origin is pca.transform."
          callouts={[
            { kind: 'mark', r: 7, c: 102, label: '1', tone: 'red' },
            { kind: 'ring', r: 19, c: 5, w: 85, h: 1, tone: 'red' },
            { kind: 'mark', r: 19, c: 94, label: '2', tone: 'red' },
            { kind: 'ring', r: 20, c: 5, w: 85, h: 2, tone: 'amber' },
            { kind: 'mark', r: 20.5, c: 94, label: '3', tone: 'amber' },
            { kind: 'ring', r: 28, c: 3, w: 144, h: 1, tone: 'red' },
          ]}
        />
        <ol class="legend-list">
          <li><span class="n red">1</span><b>Incident.</b> Two requests after the fault.</li>
          <li><span class="n red">2</span><b>Likely origin.</b> pca.transform: 2.79 → 14.55.</li>
          <li><span class="n amber">3</span><b>The damage spreads.</b> Downstream steps follow within seconds.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 09 INVESTIGATE ============ -->
    <section id="investigate" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">09</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">An incident, and the assistant <span class="serif accent">tells the story</span>.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="split">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              The second step works from the frozen incident: the affected part of the pipeline, its baselines, the current
              values and the likely origin. In this run we started it with <em>Investigate</em>; it can also start by itself
              as soon as an incident is raised.
            </p>
            <ol class="legend-list legend-list--stacked">
              <li><span class="n">1</span><b>Evidence, not guesses.</b> It confirms <code>pca.transform</code> with numbers: mean, spread and maximum of its output before and after.</li>
              <li><span class="n">2</span><b>A plain-language diagnosis.</b> Inputs and runtime are healthy, so it rules out a data or infrastructure fault, and says how to verify recovery.</li>
              <li><span class="n amber">3</span><b>A concrete fix, held for approval.</b> It proposes rolling the live model back from version 7 to version 6. A rollback is a critical action, so it waits for a person to choose <em>Execute</em>.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
              The diagnosis is a reasonable reading of the evidence: from the outside, a broken PCA step in the live model
              version looks exactly like a bad deployment. In this demo we know the real cause, so instead of rolling back we
              remove the injected fault.
            </p>
          </div>
          <TermCapture
            name="investigate"
            cols={75}
            max={15}
            title="mlox tui · Pipeline Observer · remediation proposal"
            label="The remediation proposal: an assessment that locates the fault at pca.transform, a verification step, and Add to Plan, Execute and Dismiss buttons."
            callouts={[
              { kind: 'mark', r: 2, c: 73, label: '1' },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 11, c: 73, label: '2' },
              { kind: 'mark', r: 15, c: 73, label: '3', tone: 'amber' },
            ]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ 10 RECOVER ============ -->
    <section id="recover" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">10</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Fault off, windows drain, <span class="serif accent">recovered</span>.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              Typing <code>off</code> restores the PCA step. MLOX does not take that on faith: the state turns to
              <em>recovered</em> only when every step is back within its baseline across its windows, here about 30 seconds after
              the fix.
            </p>
            <TermCapture
              name="corrupt_off"
              cols={54}
              max={13}
              label="The traffic simulator after typing off: fault injection is off again."
            />
            <ol class="legend-list legend-list--stacked">
              <li><span class="n">1</span><b>Proof, not hope.</b> Recovered only when every step is back within its baseline.</li>
              <li><span class="n">2</span><b>Keep what was learned.</b> <em>Save Postmortem</em> writes the incident into the project's knowledge base; the assistant's proposal is already on the Operations plan.</li>
            </ol>
          </div>
          <TermCapture
            name="recovered"
            cols={149}
            max={11}
            title="mlox tui · Operations › Pipelines"
            label="The pipeline in the recovered state with all steps back to normal."
            callouts={[{ kind: 'mark', r: 7, c: 104, label: '1' }, { kind: 'mark', r: 0, c: 131, label: '2' }]}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ TRY IT ============ -->
    <section id="try" class="chapter">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="chapter__head">
          <p class="chapter__num mono">11</p>
          <h2 class="h-sub">Try it <span class="serif accent">yourself</span>.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="split split--code">
          <div class="chapter__text">
            <p>
              You need an MLOX project with running MLflow, OpenTelemetry Collector and MLflow Gateway services, with the
              collector bound to the gateway. The reasoning model is optional for detection; without it you get incidents and
              likely origins, but no investigation. Remember this is experimental: use a test project.
            </p>
            <p>
              The full walkthrough, including the telemetry contract and how to debug an empty Pipelines view, is in the
              <a href={`${repo}/tree/main/examples/operations_demo`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">demo README</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <pre class="snippet"><code><span class="c"># point the examples at your project</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export MLOX_PROJECT_PATH="/path/to/project.mlox"
<span class="p">$</span> export MLOX_PROJECT_PASSWORD="…"

<span class="c"># register the model and set its champion alias</span>
<span class="p">$</span> uv run -m examples.operations_demo.register

<span class="c"># TUI: Operations › Settings › Start Watching, then</span>
<span class="p">$</span> uv run -m examples.operations_demo.traffic
<span class="p">operations-demo&gt;</span> on    <span class="c"># inject the fault</span>
<span class="p">operations-demo&gt;</span> off   <span class="c"># repair it</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ============ NEXT ============ -->
